<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter9_pointer-variables-and-dereferencing">
    <title>Pointer Variables and Dereferencing</title>

    <p>
        A pointer declaration includes the type of object that the pointer is
        expected to identify. The <c>*</c> in a declaration says that the new
        variable is a pointer.
    </p>

    <program xml:id="program_ch9_pointer_declaration_and_dereference"
             language="cpp" interactive="yes" line-numbers="yes">
        <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    int score = 90;
    int* score_pointer = &score;

    std::cout << *score_pointer << '\n';

    *score_pointer = 95;
    std::cout << score << '\n';

    return 0;
}
        ]]></code>
    </program>

    <p>
        In <c>int* score_pointer</c>, the <c>*</c> in line 5 is part of the declaration
        syntax(C++ grammar); * here is not an operator. It says that <c>score_pointer</c> has type pointer to
        <c>int</c>. In <c>*score_pointer</c>, the <c>*</c> is the dereference
        operator. It follows the stored address and accesses the object there.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="table_ch9_pointer_symbols">
        <title>Two pointer-related operators</title>
        <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
            <col width="25%"/>
            <col width="37%"/>
            <col width="38%"/>
            <row header="yes">
                <cell>Expression</cell>
                <cell>Role</cell>
                <cell>Meaning</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell><c>int* pointer</c></cell>
                <cell>Pointer declaration</cell>
                <cell>Declares <c>pointer</c> as a pointer to an <c>int</c>.</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell><c>&amp;score</c></cell>
                <cell>Address-of operator</cell>
                <cell>Produces the address of <c>score</c>.</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
                <cell><c>*pointer</c></cell>
                <cell>Dereference operator</cell>
                <cell>Accesses the object identified by <c>pointer</c>.</cell>
            </row>
        </tabular>
    </table>

    <p>
        These declarations are equivalent:
    </p>

    <program xml:id="program_ch9_pointer_declaration_spacing"
             language="cpp">
        <code><![CDATA[
int* first_pointer;
int *second_pointer;
        ]]></code>
    </program>

    <p>
        In this course, we will declare one variable per line. This avoids a
        common source of confusion:
    </p>

    <program xml:id="program_ch9_one_pointer_per_line"
             language="cpp">
        <code><![CDATA[
int* first_pointer;
int second_value;
        ]]></code>
    </program>

    <p>
        In <c>int* first_pointer, second_value;</c>, only
        <c>first_pointer</c> is a pointer. The second variable is an ordinary
        <c>int</c>. One declaration per line makes this distinction visible.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter9_pointer-variables-and-dereferencing_pointer-storage">
        <title>What a Pointer Contains and Where It Is Stored</title>

        <p>
            Consider this code:
        </p>

        <program xml:id=""
                 language="cpp" interactive="yes" line-numbers="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <cstdint>

int main() {
    std::int32_t score = 90;
    std::int32_t* score_pointer = &score;

    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>

        <p>
            The pointer variable and the <c>score</c> object are two different
            objects. The pointer stores the virtual address of the first byte of
            <c>score</c>. The pointer variable itself also occupies memory.
            An address is a numeric identifier; when that identifier is stored
            in an object pointer on a typical 64-bit desktop system, the pointer
            object usually occupies eight bytes.
        </p>

        <p>
            The pointer variable is stored in ordinary program memory; there is
            no separate general-purpose area called <q>pointer memory</q>. For
            example, a local pointer variable is usually stored in the function's
            stack storage, while a pointer created with <c>new</c> is itself
            stored in dynamic storage. In either case, its bytes contain a
            pointer value—the virtual address of another object. The object being
            pointed to may be in a different storage area. The pointer variable
            and its target are therefore separate objects with separate storage.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="figure_chapter9_pointer_and_target_storage">
            <caption>A pointer object and its target occupy separate storage</caption>
            <image source="Chapter9/pointer-and-target-storage-address-of-clear.svg" width="90%">
                <description>
                    <p>
                        The pointer object <c>score_pointer</c> occupies eight
                        bytes beginning at virtual address 0x0000'7000. Its
                        stored value is the virtual address 0x0000'A000. A
                        separate four-byte integer object <c>score</c> begins at
                        that address. The pointer and target are separate
                        objects with separate storage.
                    </p>
                </description>
            </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
            This figure shows a common arrangement, not a rule that every
            pointer must be on the stack or every target must be in dynamic
            storage. A pointer object can also have static storage, and its
            target can be a local object, a static object, or a dynamically
            allocated object. During execution, a compiler may temporarily keep
            a pointer value in a CPU register instead of memory. That optimization
            does not change the C++ idea: the pointer variable represents an
            object whose value identifies another object's address.
        </p>

        <p>
            When estimating storage, count the pointer object and its target as
            separate objects. For example, if <c>score</c> is a four-byte
            <c>std::int32_t</c> and <c>score_pointer</c> is an eight-byte pointer,
            their object storage totals about twelve bytes on a typical 64-bit
            system. The pointer's eight bytes store an address; they do not
            include the four bytes occupied by <c>score</c>. If the target was
            created with <c>new</c>, deleting the target releases its storage,
            but the pointer variable remains until its own lifetime ends.
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table_chapter9_pointer_storage_accounting">
            <title>Counting a pointer and its target</title>
            <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
                <col width="30%"/>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <col width="45%"/>
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell>Object</cell>
                    <cell>Typical size</cell>
                    <cell>What the storage contains</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><c>score</c></cell>
                    <cell>4 bytes</cell>
                    <cell>The 32-bit integer value</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><c>score_pointer</c></cell>
                    <cell>8 bytes</cell>
                    <cell>The virtual address of <c>score</c></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Both objects</cell>
                    <cell>About 12 bytes</cell>
                    <cell>The target and the pointer are counted separately</cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
            The phrase <q>64-bit architecture</q> is connected to this example
            because the machine commonly represents an object address in a
            64-bit pointer, which is typically eight bytes. It does not mean
            that a memory cell is 64 bits, that an <c>int</c> is 64 bits, or
            that every object occupies eight bytes.
        </p>

        <interactive xml:id="interactive_chapter9_pointer_storage"
                     iframe="https://pythontutor.com/iframe-embed.html#code=%23include%20%3Ccstdint%3E%0A%23include%20%3Ciomanip%3E%0A%23include%20%3Ciomanip%3E%0A%23include%20%3Ciostream%3E%0A%0Aint%20main%28%29%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Aint32_t%20score%20%3D%2090%3B%0A%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Aint32_t*%20score_pointer%20%3D%20%26score%3B%0A%0A%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Acout%20%3C%3C%20%22Bytes%20in%20score%3A%20%22%20%3C%3C%20sizeof%28score%29%20%3C%3C%20%27%5Cn%27%3B%0A%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Acout%20%3C%3C%20%22Bytes%20in%20pointer%3A%20%22%20%3C%3C%20sizeof%28score_pointer%29%20%3C%3C%20%27%5Cn%27%3B%0A%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Acout%20%3C%3C%20%22Score%3A%20%22%20%3C%3C%20score%20%3C%3C%20%27%5Cn%27%3B%0A%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Acout%20%3C%3C%20%22Address%3A%20%22%20%3C%3C%20score_pointer%20%3C%3C%20%27%5Cn%27%3B%0A%0A%20%20%20%20return%200%3B%0A%7D&amp;codeDivHeight=400&amp;codeDivWidth=350&amp;cppShowMemAddrs=true&amp;curInstr=1&amp;origin=opt-frontend.js&amp;py=cpp_g%2B%2B9.3.0"
                     width="100%"
                     aspect="16:10">
        </interactive>

        <p>
            The visualization shows addresses assigned by the tool's simulated
            environment. Use it to see that a pointer stores an address and that
            an object occupies storage. Do not treat the displayed numeric
            addresses as portable values.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter9_pointer-variables-and-dereferencing_lvalues-and-rvalues">
        <title>Lvalues and Rvalues: From Assignment to Expression Categories</title>

        <p>
            The names <term>lvalue</term> and <term>rvalue</term> originally
            came from assignment expressions. An lvalue appeared on the
            left-hand side, while an rvalue appeared on the right-hand side:
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_ch9_lvalue_rvalue_assignment"
                 language="cpp">
            <code><![CDATA[
int score = 90;
score = 95;
            ]]></code>
        </program>

        <p>
            Modern C++ uses a more useful meaning. An lvalue identifies a
            persistent object or storage location. An rvalue is usually a
            temporary value or computation result. The expression remains an
            lvalue even when it appears on the right side of an assignment:
        </p>

        <interactive xml:id="interactive_ch9_lvalue_rvalue_codelens"
                     iframe="https://pythontutor.com/iframe-embed.html#code=%23include%20%3Ciostream%3E%0A%0Aint%20main%28%29%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20int%20score%20%3D%2090%3B%0A%20%20%20%20int%20copy%20%3D%20score%3B%0A%0A%20%20%20%20score%20%3D%20score%20%2B%205%3B%0A%0A%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Acout%20%3C%3C%20%22copy%3D%22%20%3C%3C%20copy%20%3C%3C%20%22%2C%20score%3D%22%20%3C%3C%20score%3B%0A%0A%20%20%20%20return%200%3B%0A%7D&amp;codeDivHeight=400&amp;codeDivWidth=350&amp;cppShowMemAddrs=true&amp;curInstr=1&amp;origin=opt-frontend.js&amp;py=cpp_g%2B%2B9.3.0"
                     width="100%"
                     aspect="16:10">
        </interactive>

        <p>
            Here, <c>score</c> is an lvalue because it identifies an object,
            while <c>score + 5</c> is an rvalue because it is a temporary
            computation result. This is why the following is valid and invalid,
            respectively:
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_ch9_lvalue_address_examples"
                 language="cpp">
            <code><![CDATA[
int* pointer = &score;   // valid: score is an lvalue
// int* invalid = &(score + 5);  // invalid: the result is an rvalue
            ]]></code>
        </program>

        <p>
            This chapter uses only the practical distinction between lvalues and
            rvalues. More detailed categories, including prvalues, xvalues, and
            glvalues, can be studied later with move semantics.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <exercise label="chapter9_pointer-variables-and-dereferencing_exercise_star">
        <statement><p>What does <c>*score_pointer = 95;</c> do when <c>score_pointer</c> points to <c>score</c>?</p></statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>It changes the pointed-to <c>score</c> object to 95.</p></statement><feedback><p>Dereferencing accesses the object identified by the pointer.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>It changes the pointer's address to 95.</p></statement><feedback><p>The dereference operator accesses the pointed-to object, not the pointer's stored address.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>It creates a second pointer.</p></statement><feedback><p>A declaration, not this assignment, creates a pointer variable.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>It prints the address of <c>score</c>.</p></statement><feedback><p>Output requires <c>std::cout</c>; this statement performs assignment.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter9_pointer-variables-and-dereferencing_parsons_dereference" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide">
        <statement>
            <p>Arrange the statements to store an address and then change the pointed-to object.</p>
        </statement>
        <blocks>
            <block order="1"><cline>int score = 90;</cline></block>
            <block order="2"><cline>int* score_pointer = &amp;score;</cline></block>
            <block order="3"><cline>std::cout &lt;&lt; *score_pointer &lt;&lt; '\n';</cline></block>
            <block order="4"><cline>*score_pointer = 95;</cline></block>
            <block order="5"><cline>std::cout &lt;&lt; score &lt;&lt; '\n';</cline></block>
        </blocks>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter9_pointer-variables-and-dereferencing_exercise_address_of">
        <statement><p>What does <c>&amp;score</c> produce in an expression?</p></statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>The address of <c>score</c></p></statement><feedback><p>The address-of operator produces a pointer value identifying the object.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>The value stored in <c>score</c></p></statement><feedback><p>Writing <c>score</c> alone reads its value; <c>&amp;score</c> asks for its address.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>The object identified by <c>score</c></p></statement><feedback><p>The expression produces an address, not the object itself.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>The address of the pointer variable itself</p></statement><feedback><p><c>&amp;score</c> identifies <c>score</c>, not another pointer variable.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter9_pointer-variables-and-dereferencing_exercise_declaration_star">
        <statement><p>In <c>int* pointer</c>, what role does <c>*</c> play?</p></statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>It declares <c>pointer</c> as a pointer to an <c>int</c>.</p></statement><feedback><p>Here the star is part of the pointer declaration syntax.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>It immediately accesses the pointed-to object.</p></statement><feedback><p>Dereferencing occurs in an expression such as <c>*pointer</c> after the pointer exists.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>It obtains the address of <c>pointer</c>.</p></statement><feedback><p>The address-of operator is <c>&amp;</c>.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>It releases dynamic storage.</p></statement><feedback><p>Storage release uses <c>delete</c> or <c>delete[]</c>.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter9_pointer-variables-and-dereferencing_exercise_lvalue">
        <statement><p>Which expression is an lvalue because it identifies a persistent object?</p></statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p><c>score</c></p></statement><feedback><p>The variable name identifies a storage location and can appear on the left side of assignment.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><c>score + 5</c></p></statement><feedback><p>This computation produces a temporary rvalue.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><c>90 + 5</c></p></statement><feedback><p>A numeric computation result is an rvalue.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p><c>5</c></p></statement><feedback><p>A literal is a value, not a named storage location.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter9_pointer-variables-and-dereferencing_parsons_storage" adaptive="yes" indentation="hide">
        <statement>
            <p>Arrange the blocks to create an integer and store its address in a pointer.</p>
        </statement>
        <blocks>
            <block order="1"><cline>std::int32_t score = 90;</cline></block>
            <block order="2"><cline>std::int32_t* score_pointer = &amp;score;</cline></block>
            <block order="3"><cline>std::cout &lt;&lt; *score_pointer &lt;&lt; '\n';</cline></block>
        </blocks>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter9_pointer-variables-and-dereferencing_exercise_virtual_address">
        <statement>
            <p>What does a pointer in an ordinary C++ process normally contain?</p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>A virtual address that identifies an object in that process's address space</p></statement><feedback><p>The operating system and hardware translate virtual addresses to physical locations.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>The object's complete contents</p></statement><feedback><p>A pointer stores an address, not a copy of the object.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>A physical RAM address that is valid in every process</p></statement><feedback><p>Processes normally use separate virtual address spaces.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>The page-table entry itself</p></statement><feedback><p>Page tables are used by the system to translate addresses; a pointer is a C++ object value.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>
</section>
